### `mcp__codex_app__create_worktree`
Create and attach a managed Git worktree on this chat's host. Subagent worktrees appear in the original parent chat's attachments. On success, use the returned workspace directory explicitly in the active turn. It becomes the calling chat's working directory for subsequent turns unless the result tells you to keep using it explicitly. If writes there are denied, request write access to that workspace with request_permissions when available, or request approval for the needed command. If registration fails, call attach_worktree with its returned workspace directory as path to retry attachment and the working-directory update. Follow applicable user, repository, and skill instructions when deciding whether and how to create a worktree. Unless the user requests a new worktree, inspect list_artifacts and prefer reusing a suitable active worktree. Use archive_worktree when you no longer need a worktree created with this tool. It saves a recoverable Git snapshot of local changes and unpushed commits before removing the checkout. Starts detached at the supplied ref. Uncommitted changes are not copied. When asked to move edits, choose a base that excludes unrelated source commits. Identify the requested changes from the task history and the staged and unstaged diffs. Apply only those edits and new files to the destination's version, verify them, then remove only those edits from the source unless asked to keep them. Preserve other edits and their staging, including in the same file. If you cannot separate them safely, leave the source intact and tell the user. Returns paths when complete or an operationId to check with get_worktree_creation_status. This tool is part of plugin `codex-app-tools`.

